# Common Mistakes to Avoid

These patterns have been identified from code reviews and hotfixes. Always check for these issues.

---

## Performance: Preventing Unnecessary Re-renders

### 1. Icons Must Be Memoized

**Problem:** Importing icons directly from `@tabler/icons-react` causes unnecessary re-renders because each render creates a new component reference.

```typescript
// BAD
import { IconFileWord, IconCheck } from "@tabler/icons-react";
<IconFileWord className="h-4 w-4" />
```

```typescript
// GOOD
import { MemoIconFileWord, MemoIconCheck } from "@/submodules/react-components/components/kern-icons/icons";
<MemoIconFileWord className="h-4 w-4" />
```

**Rule:** Always use `MemoIcon*` from `kern-icons/icons.ts`. If an icon isn't available, add it there first with `memo()`.

### 2. Wrap Event Handlers in useCallback

**Problem:** Inline functions create new references on every render, causing child components to re-render.

```typescript
// BAD - new function reference every render
<KernButton onClick={() => dispatch(openModal(ModalEnum.SETTINGS))} />

// BAD - function defined in render without useCallback
const handleClick = () => dispatch(openModal(ModalEnum.SETTINGS));
<KernButton onClick={handleClick} />
```

```typescript
// GOOD - stable function reference
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    dispatch(openModal(ModalEnum.SETTINGS));
}, [dispatch]);

<KernButton onClick={handleClick} />
```

### 3. Memoize Components with memo()

**Problem:** Components re-render even when their props haven't changed.

```typescript
// BAD
export default function Sidebar(props: SidebarProps) { ... }
```

```typescript
// GOOD
function Sidebar(props: SidebarProps) { ... }
export default memo(Sidebar);
```

**When to use memo():** Sidebars, menus, toolbars, list items, and components that receive stable props but have expensive render logic.

### 4. Use useMemo for Expensive Computations

**Problem:** Expensive calculations or object/array creation runs on every render.

```typescript
// BAD - filter/map runs every render
const filteredItems = items.filter(i => i.active).map(i => i.name);

// BAD - new object every render
const config = { theme: 'dark', size: props.size };
```

```typescript
// GOOD - only recalculates when items changes
const filteredItems = useMemo(() => 
    items.filter(i => i.active).map(i => i.name),
[items]);

// GOOD - stable object reference
const config = useMemo(() => ({ 
    theme: 'dark', 
    size: props.size 
}), [props.size]);
```

**Note:** Simple expressions like `const x = a ? b : c` don't need useMemo.

### 5. Define Constants Outside Components

**Problem:** Arrays, objects, or values recreated on every render.

```typescript
// BAD - new array every render
function MyComponent() {
    const options = ['Option 1', 'Option 2'];
    return <KernDropdown options={options} />;
}
```

```typescript
// GOOD - stable reference
const OPTIONS = ['Option 1', 'Option 2'];

function MyComponent() {
    return <KernDropdown options={OPTIONS} />;
}
```

### 6. Avoid Inline Object/Array Props

**Problem:** Inline objects or arrays create new references every render.

```typescript
// BAD
<MyComponent style={{ padding: 10 }} />
<KernDropdown options={['a', 'b', 'c']} />
```

```typescript
// GOOD - define outside or useMemo
const STYLE = { padding: 10 };
const OPTIONS = ['a', 'b', 'c'];

<MyComponent style={STYLE} />
<KernDropdown options={OPTIONS} />
```

