## Code Quality

### 13. Guard Against Null/Undefined

**Problem:** Accessing properties on null/undefined values.

```typescript
// BAD
useEffect(() => {
    fetchData(user.organizationId); // crashes if user is null
}, []);
```

```typescript
// GOOD
useEffect(() => {
    if (!user?.organizationId) return;
    fetchData(user.organizationId);
}, [user?.organizationId]);
```

### 14. Import Enums from Submodules

**Problem:** Duplicate enum definitions cause inconsistencies.

```typescript
// BAD - local duplicate
import { StrategyStepType } from "@/src/util/enums";
```

```typescript
// GOOD - shared source of truth
import { StrategyStepType } from "@/submodules/javascript-functions/enums/enums";
```

### 15. Use Existing Types

**Problem:** Recreating types that already exist.

```typescript
// BAD
type MyStrategy = { id: string; name: string; steps: any[] };
```

```typescript
// GOOD
import { Strategy } from "@/src/services/strategies";
```

### 16. Use Existing Components

**Problem:** Rebuilding components that exist in submodules.

```typescript
// BAD - custom implementation
<div className="flex items-center">
    <Switch checked={value} onChange={onChange} />
    <span>{label}</span>
</div>
```

```typescript
// GOOD - use submodule component
import SwitchWithLabel from "@/submodules/react-components/components/SwitchWithLabel";
<SwitchWithLabel checked={value} onChange={onChange} label={label} />
```

**Check submodules for:** buttons, dropdowns, tables, switches, badges, modals, loading indicators.

### 17. Use `T[]` Not `Array<T>`

**Problem:** Inconsistent array type syntax.

```typescript
// BAD
const items: Array<string> = [];
const users: Array<User> = [];
```

```typescript
// GOOD
const items: string[] = [];
const users: User[] = [];
```

### 18. Only Add Generic Types When Type Can't Be Inferred

**Problem:** Unnecessary generic types when TypeScript can infer the type from the initial value.

```typescript
// BAD - unnecessary generic type
const [modelName, setModelName] = useState<string>(''); // TypeScript knows it's string
const [count, setCount] = useState<number>(0); // TypeScript knows it's number
```

```typescript
// GOOD - let TypeScript infer from initial value
const [modelName, setModelName] = useState(''); // inferred as string
const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // inferred as number

// GOOD - generic type needed when initial value doesn't specify type
const [modelsList, setModelsList] = useState<string[]>([]); // empty array needs type
const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null); // null needs type
const [data, setData] = useState<EpicObject | null>(null); // null needs type
```

**Rule:** Only add generic types when the initial value doesn't provide enough type information (empty arrays, null values, undefined).

### 19. Remove Console Logs

**Problem:** Debug logs in production code.

```typescript
// BAD
console.log('debug:', value);
console.log('user:', user);
```

```typescript
// GOOD
// Remove all console.log before committing
// Use proper error handling or logging service if needed
```

### 20. Wrap I/O in Try-Catch

**Problem:** File/network operations without error handling.

```typescript
// BAD
const reader = new FileReader();
reader.readAsText(file);
```

```typescript
// GOOD
try {
    const reader = new FileReader();
    reader.onerror = () => handleError('Failed to read file');
    reader.onload = (e) => handleSuccess(e.target?.result);
    reader.readAsText(file);
} catch (error) {
    handleError('Failed to initialize file reader');
}
```

### 21. Modal Pattern: Pass Setter Directly

**Problem:** Creating wrapper functions for simple state updates.

```typescript
// BAD - unnecessary wrapper
const handleClose = () => setOpen(false);
<Modal open={open} onClose={handleClose} />

// ALSO BAD - inline function (new reference every render)
<Modal open={open} onClose={() => setOpen(false)} />
```

```typescript
// GOOD - pass setter directly when component supports it
<Modal open={open} setOpen={setOpen} />

// GOOD - useCallback if onClose signature is required
const handleClose = useCallback(() => setOpen(false), []);
<Modal open={open} onClose={handleClose} />
```

### 22. Tooltip Cursor: Use cursor-auto for Non-Clickable Elements

**Problem:** Tooltip component defaults to `cursor-pointer`, which incorrectly suggests the element is clickable when it's only informational.

```typescript
// BAD - misleading pointer cursor on non-clickable tooltip
<Tooltip text="This is informational only">
  <span>Hover me</span>
</Tooltip>
```

```typescript
// GOOD - use cursor-auto for non-clickable tooltips
<Tooltip className="cursor-auto" text="This is informational only">
  <span>Hover me</span>
</Tooltip>
```

**Rule:** Only use the default `cursor-pointer` when the tooltip wraps a clickable element. Use `cursor-auto` for informational tooltips on non-interactive elements.

### 23. Boolean Props: Omit `={true}` and Omit Prop When `false`

**Problem:** Explicitly passing `true` for boolean props is redundant. Passing `false` is unnecessary since omitting the prop defaults to `false`.

```typescript
// BAD - redundant true values
<KernDropdown hasSearchBar={true} hasCheckboxes={true} disabled={true} />

// BAD - unnecessary false prop
<KernDropdown hasSearchBar={false} />
```

```typescript
// GOOD - omit ={true}, just use the prop name
<KernDropdown hasSearchBar hasCheckboxes disabled />

// GOOD - omit prop entirely when false
<KernDropdown /> // hasSearchBar defaults to false
```

**Rule:** For boolean props set to `true`, use the prop name without `={true}`. For `false`, omit the prop entirely.

